":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":4,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Liquid lithium at 800 K has a self-diffusion coefficient of **D = (2.2 ± 0.4) × 10⁻⁴ cm²/s** with the MACE-MP-0 model. The NVE energy drift is negligible, and a check run near the melting point lands within 13% of the measured value.\n\n**Premise check.** The question is well-posed. Lithium melts at 453.7 K, so it is well into the liquid at 800 K. MACE-MP-0 was trained on data that includes Li. The diffusion coefficient comes from how far atoms wander over time (the mean squared displacement): over the 20 ps run it reached about 129 Å² and grew linearly with time (log-log slope 1.03), so the run was long enough.\n\n**Setup.** NVIDIA ALCHEMI Toolkit with MACE-MP-0 medium, 250 atoms, fixed at the measured density of 0.4832 g/cm³ (Ohse 1985, via the Yamdb compilation).\n- Melted for 3 ps at 1500 K, then equilibrated for 5 ps at 800 K with a Langevin thermostat.\n- Production: 20 ps of NVE with velocity Verlet, 1 fs timestep.\n- D came from the slope of the averaged mean squared displacement, with the system's centre-of-mass drift removed.\n\n**Energy drift.** Total energy drifted by 1.3 × 10⁻⁴ meV/atom/ps and stayed within a 0.016 meV/atom band over the 20 ps. The average temperature during NVE was 797 ± 29 K.\n\n**Validation.** I ran the same setup aimed at 470 K; the temperature settled at 458 K once the thermostat was off. It gave D = 5.9 × 10⁻⁵ cm²/s. The NMR measurement (Murday & Cotts, J. Chem. Phys. 48, 4938, 1968) is 6.8 ± 0.3 × 10⁻⁵ cm²/s at the melting point, so the model comes out about 13% low. Correcting for the small simulation box would raise the simulated values and close most of that gap.\n- As a separate comparison, extending the paper's temperature fit (D = 0.94 × 10⁻³ exp(−2300 cal/mol / RT)) to 800 K gives about 2.2 × 10⁻⁴ cm²/s, matching my result. That is an extrapolation beyond the temperatures they measured.\n- I took the Murday & Cotts numbers from a search summary of the abstract; both the ADS and ResearchGate pages refused the fetch, so I couldn't read the paper itself.\n\n**What the ± 0.4 × 10⁻⁴ covers:**\n- **Statistics:** about ± 0.1 × 10⁻⁴, from splitting the run into four 5 ps blocks (block values 1.7–2.1 × 10⁻⁴).\n- **Box size:** the correction for the small box was not applied. It would raise D by roughly 10–15%, but it needs a viscosity value and I had no sourced one.\n- **Model error:** about 15–20%, judged from the validation run.\n\nThe ± 0.4 × 10⁻⁴ does not cover whether the model's own density at 800 K matches experiment (I did not check that, since the density was fixed), or anything that a single 250-atom trajectory might miss.\n\n**On the board:**\n- candidate `8fe243a6` (fulfils need #0)\n- property_result `71a3c7dc` (`self_diffusion`, cm2/s, no flags; caveats are in the note)\n- check `e4dfb532` (literature_anchor: agree)\n\nSources:\n- [Murday & Cotts, Self-Diffusion Coefficient of Liquid Lithium (ADS)](http://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1968JChPh..48.4938M)\n- [ResearchGate entry for the same paper](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/289594580_Self-diffusion_coefficient_of_liquid_lithium)\n- [Yamdb: thermophysical properties of liquid metals (arXiv:2402.10922)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.10922)\n- [OSTI: QENS diffusion in liquid Li](https://www.osti.gov/etdeweb/biblio/21180951)\n- [OSTI: Self-diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium](https://www.osti.gov/etdeweb/biblio/21547860)","ttft_ms":2209,"type":"result","duration_ms":39724,"uuid":"6549408c-082a-4725-ac5e-5df86b64bc9f","ttft_stream_ms":1188,"time_to_request_ms":56,"first_content_frame_ms":1189,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":1,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":8,"outputTokens":4086,"cacheReadInputTokens":203455,"cacheCreationInputTokens":53521,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.39004799999999995,"contextWin